--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0593 was ruled a monster on 2026-09-02 and is now the tracker for the
attachment-format estate. This is its first child and the only one that needs no
machine: the fixture matrix its Action 1 calls for, plus the content-capability
classifier that decides a file's format from its bytes rather than from its
name.

The matrix has to exist before anything can be measured, and it has to exist
independently of Zotero, because what ticket 0600 measures is Zotero's behaviour
against a substrate whose contents are already known. A fixture whose format is
asserted rather than detected would make the measurement circular: Zotero would
be graded against a claim about the file rather than against the file.

Machine-free by construction. No Zotero, no converter, no network, no
subprocess, so this runs in the fast tier of `make check`.

## Relevant files

- `bench/fixtures/make_attachment_fixtures.py` — the committed generator, one
  writer per format, deterministic bytes.
- `bench/attachment_format.py` — the content-capability classifier.
- `tests/test_attachment_format_fixtures.py` — the controls.
- `bench/fixtures/make_index_fixture.mjs` — the shape to follow: a committed
  generator that writes a substrate a guard can be driven against in both
  directions, deterministic, with the omissions stated rather than faked.

## Actions

1. Write a deterministic generator producing one minimal, valid file per format,
   each carrying a unique body token so a later pass can separate real
   extraction from a metadata-only match.
2. Write a classifier that decides format from bytes alone: signatures for the
   flat formats, and for the ZIP family the `mimetype` member and the entry
   names that separate EPUB from DOCX from ODT.
3. Assert each fixture carries its token, and that the classifier answers
   correctly on each.
4. Assert the discriminating control: a fixture renamed to a misleading
   extension is still classified correctly by content, while a three-line
   extension-only classifier written inside the test gets it wrong. That control
   is the mechanical form of the tracker's invariant that the policy is
   MIME/capability based and not an extension allow-list.
5. Record every format that could not be produced faithfully as a named gap, in
   this ticket and in the generator's docstring, and carry it into ticket 0600
   as an open item.

## Test

- The misleading-extension control must be seen RED against the naive
  extension-only classifier before it is trusted green against the content one.
  A check that has never been seen red has not been shown to work.
- The ZIP family needs its own control: EPUB, DOCX and ODT share the
  `PK\x03\x04` signature, and a classifier answering `docx` for all three would
  pass a one-sided test. Assert three different answers, not three successes.

## The stated gap: legacy `.doc`

Legacy `.doc` is an OLE2 compound binary whose `WordDocument` stream is a
File Information Block with a byte layout no hand-written fixture reproduces,
and ticket 0593 does not authorize a converter to produce one. It is therefore
NOT in the matrix. Eight honest fixtures and one declared gap are worth more
than nine of which one is fiction: a fake `.doc` that no real `.doc` resembles
would make every `.doc` row of ticket 0600's measurement a statement about the
fixture rather than about the format.

The classifier still recognises the OLE2 compound-document signature and answers
`ole-compound`, the container family `.doc` belongs to. It does not answer
`doc`, because separating a Word binary from an Excel one needs stream
inspection this has no fixture to exercise. Ticket 0600 carries the gap: either
a real `.doc` is supplied from outside the repository, in which case the
classifier gains the branch and its positive control together, or the `.doc` row
of the policy is ruled on the format's documented behaviour with the absence
recorded.

Markdown is a second, milder case of the same honesty. Markdown has no content
signature: it is text, and the classifier says `text`. That is not a defect to
be patched with a heuristic, it is the finding ticket 0593 anticipated when it
required Markdown to be probed separately, and it makes what ticket 0600
observes the only answer available: whether Zotero exposes a `.md` attachment as
`text/plain` is a fact about Zotero, not about the bytes.

## Verification

Every box below is ticked against `tests/test_attachment_format_fixtures.py` and
the transcripts in pull request 233, and each was independently re-executed by
that pull request's reviewer rather than read from its body.

- [x] Each fixture's bytes are valid for its format: the ZIP members open, the
      EPUB and ODT `mimetype` entries are first and stored, the PDF's cross
      reference offsets point at its object headers.
- [x] Each fixture carries its unique token.
- [x] The classifier answers correctly on every fixture, by content.
- [x] The misleading-extension control fails the naive classifier and passes the
      content one, and the red half is recorded in the pull request.
- [x] The three ZIP-container formats get three different answers.
- [x] The generator is deterministic: two runs produce identical bytes.
- [x] No new dependency: `requirements-check.txt` is unchanged and the
      classifier is stdlib only.
- [x] `make check` and `./tickets/erg check` pass.

## Invariants

See tracker 0593 §Invariants — inherited unchanged.

Child-specific consequence, which is this ticket's own and not a restatement:
nothing here decides policy. What a format's classification MEANS for
eligibility is ticket 0600's ruling, and this ticket must not anticipate it in
code or in prose.

## Exit criteria

- [x] The generator and the classifier are committed and run in the fast tier.
- [x] Every format named by ticket 0593 is either a faithful fixture or a
      declared, named gap with its reason.
- [x] The misleading-extension control has been seen red.
- [ ] Ticket 0600 can run against this matrix without producing a file.

The last box stays unticked, and it is a criterion this ticket cannot decide
about itself rather than work left undone. What is deliverable here is the
capability: `--output-dir` writes the whole matrix from the committed generator,
so ticket 0600 authors no file. Whether that is ENOUGH for ticket 0600 is
answered by ticket 0600's first run and by nothing else, so ticking it here
would be a verification claim nobody made. It was mis-specified as an exit
criterion of this ticket; it is left standing rather than deleted so ticket
0600's first run has somewhere to land.
